# The spool transport — the mesh over a file medium (store-and-forward)

The `spool` transport runs the whole mesh (invite, handshake, routing, E2E) over
a **shared directory** instead of a socket. It is the substrate for
store-and-forward: the directory can live on removable media (a USB stick).

URL scheme: `spool://<directory_path>`
(e.g. `spool:///mnt/usb/mesh`, `spool://./link`).

## How it works

A link = a directory containing, per session, two append-only journal files, one
per direction:

```
<link>/sess-<random>/
    c2s.spool   client → server
    s2c.spool   server → client
```

- **Client** (`node.join("spool://<link>", code)`): creates a session
  subdirectory, writes to `c2s.spool`, reads `s2c.spool`.
- **Server** (`node.start(["spool://<link>"])`): watches the directory, creates
  one transport per new session it detects (multi-client), writes `s2c.spool`,
  reads `c2s.spool`.

Each packet is written as a `magic | length | crc32 | payload` record, and
**fsync**ed (it survives a power cut or the stick being pulled). The reader
tracks an offset and **resynchronises** on corruption (the medium is hostile): a
record with an invalid CRC is skipped, not fatal.

When both ends are online on the same directory, the link behaves like a live
(if asynchronous) connection. Carrying a file offline to another machine is the
delay-tolerant case.

## A portable container: `Bundle`

To carry a batch of packets as a single file (the "USB stick file"),
`src/spool.py` provides `Bundle`:

```python
from src.spool import write_bundle, read_bundle
write_bundle("/mnt/usb/carry.bundle", [pkt.pack() for pkt in packets])
raw_packets = read_bundle("/mnt/usb/carry.bundle")   # raises BundleError if corrupt
```

SHA-256 integrity over the whole container: any truncation or alteration on the
medium is **detected and rejected**. Confidentiality and authenticity stay at
the packet level (already E2E encrypted/authenticated) — the carrier can neither
read nor forge.

## Security

- The medium is **untrusted**: every packet carried is already E2E encrypted,
  and the node's hardened receive path rejects any malformed or forged packet.
- Integrity at the record level (CRC) and at the container level (SHA-256).
- Durable writes (fsync on the file and on the directory).

## Known limitation

`TransportManager` accepts **only one server per scheme**. One node therefore
cannot listen on two distinct `spool://` directories at once (the
A—usb1—B—usb2—C topology). The current workaround: several clients join the same
host directory (`SpoolServer` is multi-client). Multi-listener support per
scheme: see ROADMAP.
